Diophantus of Alexandria was an ancient Greek mathematician, 
often referred to as the "father of algebra." He lived around 
200–284 AD, although exact dates of his life are uncertain. 
His most significant work is the "Arithmetica," a series of
books in which he studied and solved algebraic equations, 
particularly what are now known as Diophantine equations—
polynomial equations that seek integer solutions. Diophantus’ 
pioneering work transformed problem-solving in mathematics and 
remains influential, particularly in number theory and algebra.

Diophantine Equations: He focused on equations with integer 
solutions, laying early groundwork for number theory.

Symbolic Notation: While his notation was not as modern as 
today’s algebra symbols, he used symbols and abbreviations 
to simplify complex calculations. This early system, sometimes 
called syncopated algebra, predates modern algebraic symbols 
and provides a structured way to represent unknowns, powers, 
and operations. It focused on rational rather than integer 
solutions, although it inspired the broader development of 
Diophantine equations studied today.

Core Features of Diophantine Symbolic Notation

Unknowns:
Represented by Greek letters, often contractions of the word 
arithmos ("number"). For example, the unknown symbol is ς (ς).

Powers of the Unknown:
Second power (square): ΔΥ, from Greek dunamis (strength/power)
Cube: KΥ, from Greek kubos (cube)

Higher powers used similar conventions, e.g. ΔΥΔ (square-
square), ΔKΥ (square-cube), KΥK (cube-cube)

Coefficients:
Coefficients are written after the variable (opposite of
modern notation). For constants, symbol M^∘ (M) was used (Greek
monades, units)

Operations:

Addition: Denoted by juxtaposition; terms written consecutively 
are added. Example: αβ represents α+β

Subtraction: Represented by a specialized symbol, an inverted 
capital lambda with a superimposed iota (ΛI), derived from 
Greek leipis ("lacking"). 

Multiplication & Division: Explicit multiplication symbols 
were omitted; division handled by fractions with notation 
like ’νμρ’ιω indicating "divided by"

Equation Structure:

Diophantus did not use an equals sign in our modern sense; 
he wrote equations in rhetorical form with the symbol ἴσ 
(short for ἴσος, meaning "equals"). Coefficients and powers 
are placed in strict procedural sequence: power first, then 
coefficient.

